  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nMid level water vapor shows troughing to the northwest with the \nridge in place over the Four Corners region, poised to weaken into \nTuesday as the trough becomes more pronounced. Minor to moderate \nHeatRisk continues today for much of the region, although \ntemperatures begin a cooling trend today, reaching near normal by \nTuesday afternoon. High clouds will continue to move into the region \ntoday as southeasterly flow shunts moisture northward, but these \nclouds shouldn't meaningfully limit storm potential in the mountains \nthis afternoon. The morning sounding indicated decent CAPE and \nincreasing PWs of 1.3-1.6\", with models signaling a wide swath of \ninstability to set up today stretching from southwestern Arizona \ninto southeastern California. This instability, along with the ample \nmoisture in the mid and lower levels, will be key for more \nwidespread monsoonal thunderstorms today, likely kicking off as \nearly as noon. While much of the convection today will be \nanchored to the mountains, a few storms may migrate into the \ndeserts and/or far eastern valleys with brief, heavy rainfall. \nRain rates in the higher terrain will be a bit more impressive \ntoday, with 1\"/hr or higher not out of the question for the San \nDiego and Riverside county mountains. Further north in San \nBernardino, there will be a significant cut off of the moisture \nnear the border with LA County. Therefore, lesser max rain rates \ncloser to 0.5-0.75\"/hr can be expected, with the strongest storms \nlikely on the eastern/northeastern slopes of the SB County \nmountains. While widespread flooding isn't expected, some \nlocalized spots, especially near the peaks or eastern sides of the\nmountains, could see some brief flooding both today and tomorrow.\nAs always with any thunderstorm, frequent lightning, gusty winds,\nand small hail will be possible. Ensure you are prepared to take \nshelter in a sturdy and enclosed structure should you hear \nthunder. \n\nThe ridge continues to weaken on Tuesday with some models now \nindicating a weak easterly wave may move across Tuesday morning, \nwhich could allow for some morning rainfall for the deserts and far \ninland areas. Should this come to pass, a rumble of thunder or two \ncannot be ruled out. This will be something to closely watch as any \nmorning convection could limit afternoon storms in the mountains. As \nit stands now, additional moisture will be pushed into the region on \nTuesday with a slight decrease in the instability, allowing for \nanother round of monsoonal thunderstorms Tuesday afternoon. \nConditions look conducive for a rince-and-repeat of Monday, but with \nthe potential for slightly higher rain rates/localized flooding as \nPWs peak above 1.5\". Again, much of this will depend on what occurs \nTuesday morning with any easterly wave, but for now, will continue \nto message widespread mountain thunderstorms for Tuesday afternoon. \n\nAfter Tuesday, the aforementioned upper level trough will encompass \nmuch of California as the state experiences a significant cooldown, \nwith temperatures falling to 3-10 degrees below normal Wed-Fri and \nthe marine layer deepens each day. This will be the first time in \nnearly a month that highs will be below normal, so take advantage of \nthe cooler weather! Monsoonal moisture will linger in the region \nwhich could allow for some abnormally warmer mornings, but with \nlimited forcing, afternoon thunderstorm chances will be falling \nthrough the end of the week. Still, models continue to push for a \nfew storms over the San Bernardinos Wednesday afternoon as the \ntrough begins to move in. Otherwise, quieter weather for the end of \nthe week and weekend with cooler weather/a more robust marine layer \nand afternoon breezy winds in the deserts and mountain passes.",
